      "generated_answer": "The Chartist movement in 19th-century Britain primarily advocated for political reform, specifically extending voting rights to working-class men. The excerpt criticizes Chartists for their radical tactics and references \"Household Suffrage,\" which relates to voting rights, indicating the central demand was for broader male suffrage. Although the movement had diverse elements, its core demand was universal male suffrage, not including women or broader social reforms.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
