      "generated_answer": "The first passage emphasizes the obligation of jihad as a continuous duty until Islam prevails globally and criticizes rulers who make peace with non-Muslims, highlighting the seriousness with which Muslim elites viewed holy war. This supports the idea that the ideology of jihad was not merely defensive but a central religious and political imperative. Therefore, the passage most strongly supports the assumption that Muslim elites took the ideology of holy war seriously.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
