      "generated_answer": "The first passage from *The Epic of Gilgamesh* describes the afterlife as a dark, desolate place devoid of light and joy, reflecting a grim view of the afterlife. In contrast, *The Maxims of Ptahhotep* emphasizes divine guidance, moral order, and a hopeful sense that those favored by the gods will not be lost, suggesting a more positive and optimistic outlook. This comparison best supports the conclusion that ancient Egyptians had a less gloomy or more optimistic view of the afterlife than ancient Sumerians.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
