      "generated_answer": "Dorothea Dix's advocacy for the humane treatment of the mentally ill was driven by moral reform and religious conviction, key characteristics of the Second Great Awakening, which inspired many social reform movements in the early to mid-19th century. Her appeal to conscience and justice, framed in moral and religious terms, aligns closely with the ethos of that religious revival. \n\n\\boxed{E}",
