    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4733",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: By analyzing the levels of 13C in bones, researchers can establish whether an individual ate:\n\nOptions: \nA. a diet high in calcium or low in calcium.\nB. a diet high in saturated fats or low in saturated fats.\nC. mostly grains or mostly nuts and fruits.\nD. mostly root vegetables or mostly leafy greens.\nE. a diet rich in sugar or low in sugar.\nF. a certain type of honey produced only in sub-Saharan Africa.\nG. mostly meat or mostly vegetables.\nH. mostly fish or mostly poultry.\nI. no dairy or lots of dairy.\nJ. mostly processed foods or mostly organic foods.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The analysis of 13C (carbon-13) isotope levels in bones is used in paleodietary studies to distinguish between different photosynthetic pathways in plants—specifically C3 and C4 plants. This helps determine whether an individual consumed more C4 plants (like maize or millet) versus C3 plants (like wheat or rice), or animals that ate such plants. This primarily differentiates diets based on plant types and trophic levels, most closely aligning with plant-based food sources like grains versus other vegetation.\n\nHowever, the best available option related to broad dietary sources detectable via 13C is the distinction between diets based on plant types such as grains (often C4) versus nuts and fruits (typically C3).\n\n\\boxed{C}",
