      "generated_answer": "The passage describes the narrator's excitement upon seeing the mobilization notice, stating that \"It's singing, sir!\" when asked what Paris was saying, and Clemenceau responds, \"It will be all right then,\" indicating a positive, enthusiastic public mood. This suggests that the people of Paris greeted the mobilization with eagerness and emotional uplift rather than fear, sorrow, skepticism, or indifference. Therefore, the best inference is that mobilization was greeted with great enthusiasm in Paris.\n\n\\boxed{J}",
