    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_211",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nYou are given an undirected graph G with N vertices and M edges.\r\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, M, the i-th edge is an undirected edge connecting vertices u_i and v_i.\nA graph with N vertices is called good if the following condition holds for all i = 1, 2, \\ldots, K:\n\n- there is no path connecting vertices x_i and y_i in G.\n\nThe given graph G is good.\nYou are given Q independent questions. Answer all of them.\r\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, Q, the i-th question is as follows.\n\n- Is the graph G^{{(i)}} obtained by adding an undirected edge connecting vertices p_i and q_i to the given graph G good?\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\r\nu_1 v_1\r\nu_2 v_2\r\n\\vdots\r\nu_M v_M\r\nK\r\nx_1 y_1\r\nx_2 y_2\r\n\\vdots\r\nx_K y_K\r\nQ\r\np_1 q_1\r\np_2 q_2\r\n\\vdots\r\np_Q q_Q\n\nOutput\n\nPrint Q lines.\r\nFor i = 1, 2, \\ldots, Q, the i-th line should contain the answer to the i-th question: Yes if the graph G^{{(i)}} is good, and No otherwise.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 0 \\leq M \\leq 2 \\times10^5\n- 1 \\leq u_i, v_i \\leq N\n- 1 \\leq K \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq x_i, y_i \\leq N\n- x_i \\neq y_i\n- i \\neq j \\implies \\lbrace x_i, y_i \\rbrace \\neq \\lbrace x_j, y_j \\rbrace\n- For all i = 1, 2, \\ldots, K, there is no path connecting vertices x_i and y_i.\n- 1 \\leq Q \\leq 2 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq p_i, q_i \\leq N\n- p_i \\neq q_i\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6 6\r\n1 2\r\n2 3\r\n2 3\r\n3 1\r\n5 4\r\n5 5\r\n3\r\n1 5\r\n2 6\r\n4 3\r\n4\r\n2 5\r\n2 6\r\n5 6\r\n5 4\n\nSample Output 1\n\nNo\r\nNo\r\nYes\r\nYes\r\n\n\n- For the first question, the graph G^{{(1)}} is not good because it has a path 1 \\rightarrow 2 \\rightarrow 5 connecting vertices x_1 = 1 and y_1 = 5. Therefore, print No.\n- For the second question, the graph G^{{(2)}} is not good because it has a path 2 \\rightarrow 6 connecting vertices x_2 = 2 and y_2 = 6. Therefore, print No.\n- For the third question, the graph G^{{(3)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n- For the fourth question, the graph G^{{(4)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n\nAs seen in this sample input, note that the given graph G may have self-loops or multi-edges.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-30b-a3b-instruct-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    from collections import defaultdict, deque\n    \n    # Read input\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    # Parse first line: N, M\n    idx = 0\n    N = int(data[idx])\n    M = int(data[idx + 1])\n    idx += 2\n    \n    # Read M edges\n    edges = []\n    graph = defaultdict(list)\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(data[idx])\n        v = int(data[idx + 1])\n        idx += 2\n        if u == v:  # self-loop, can be ignored for connectivity\n            continue\n        edges.append((u, v))\n        graph[u].append(v)\n        graph[v].append(u)\n    \n    # Read K: number of forbidden pairs\n    K = int(data[idx])\n    idx += 1\n    forbidden = []\n    for _ in range(K):\n        x = int(data[idx])\n        y = int(data[idx + 1])\n        idx += 2\n        forbidden.append((x, y))\n    \n    # Read Q: number of queries\n    Q = int(data[idx])\n    idx += 1\n    queries = []\n    for _ in range(Q):\n        p = int(data[idx])\n        q = int(data[idx + 1])\n        idx += 2\n        queries.append((p, q))\n    \n    # Step 1: Find connected components in original graph\n    visited = [False] * (N + 1)\n    components = {}\n    comp_id = 0\n    comp_map = [0] * (N + 1)  # comp_map[i] = component id of vertex i\n    \n    for i in range(1, N + 1):\n        if not visited[i]:\n            comp_id += 1\n            queue = deque([i])\n            visited[i] = True\n            while queue:\n                node = queue.popleft()\n                comp_map[node] = comp_id\n                for neighbor in graph[node]:\n                    if not visited[neighbor]:\n                        visited[neighbor] = True\n                        queue.append(neighbor)\n    \n    # Step 2: For each forbidden pair, check if they are in the same component\n    # Since original graph is good, no forbidden pair should be in the same component\n    # But just validating for correctness\n    for x, y in forbidden:\n        if comp_map[x] == comp_map[y]:\n            # This should not happen as per problem, but included for safety\n            # However, we still need to answer queries\n            pass\n    \n    # Step 3: For each query, check if adding (p, q) creates a path between any forbidden pair\n    # Adding edge (p, q) merges two components (if different)\n    # So we check: for any forbidden pair (x_i, y_i), if they were originally in different components,\n    # but the new edge connects the two components (or their union), then after adding (p,q), they become connected?\n    # So: if x_i and y_i are in different components, and the new edge connects the components containing x_i and y_i,\n    # or more precisely: if the new edge connects two vertices such that it creates a bridge between the component of x_i and that of y_i?\n    # But note: the new edge might connect two components, and if x_i and y_i are in different components now, and the new edge\n    # connects them indirectly (possibly via the new edge), then after adding, they become connected.\n    \n    # So: after adding edge (p, q), the resulting graph has component_id updated:\n    # if p and q are in different components, we merge them.\n    # So we can simulate that the new component of a vertex is the merged component of p and q (if they were in distinct ones)\n    \n    # For each query:\n    # - if p and q are in the same component already: no change -> remains good if original was good\n    # - else: they will be merged. Then we need to check for any forbidden pair (x, y) that are now in the same component\n    #   after merging comp_map[p] and comp_map[q] as one component.\n    #   But note: if a forbidden pair (x,y) are already in the same component, then it's bad -> but original graph is good,\n    #   so no forbidden pair is in same component => so we only need to worry about pairs that become connected due to this merge.\n    #   So: if x and y were in different components, and now they are in same component (because the merge connects their components),\n    #   then the new graph is not good.\n    \n    # However: the merge only connects the component of p and q.\n    # So: for a forbidden pair (x, y) to become connected, it must be that:\n    # - x is in comp_map[p] or comp_map[q] and y is in the other, OR\n    # - x and y were in different components, and the new edge connects their components?\n    # Actually: the new edge connects comp_map[p] and comp_map[q]. So any vertex in comp_map[p] becomes connected to any vertex in comp_map[q].\n    # Therefore: any forbidden pair (x, y) such that:\n    #   (x in comp_map[p] and y in comp_map[q]) OR (x in comp_map[q] and y in comp_map[p])\n    # will now be connected.\n    \n    # BUT: we must also consider that a forbidden pair (x, y) is not allowed to be connected in the new graph.\n    # So: if p and q are in different components, then the new edge connects the two components.\n    # So we must check: does there exist any forbidden pair (x, y) such that:\n    #   (x is in comp_map[p] and y is in comp_map[q]) or (x in comp_map[q] and y in comp_map[p])?\n    # But note: we must be careful: what if x and y are the same as p and q? or one of them?\n    # Actually: we care about the pair of components.\n    \n    # So: if p and q are in the same component: no change in connectivity => the graph remains good (since original was good) => answer \"Yes\"\n    # if p and q are in different components: then we check if any forbidden pair (x_i, y_i) has one endpoint in comp_map[p] and the other in comp_map[q]\n    #   if yes -> then after adding edge, x_i and y_i become connected -> bad -> \"No\"\n    #   if no -> still good -> \"Yes\"\n    \n    # However: we must note that the graph might have multiple connections, but we are only adding one edge.\n    # So we can precompute: for each component, store the set of forbidden pairs that have one endpoint in this component?\n    # But we cannot iterate over all K pairs for each query (Q up to 2e5, K up to 2e5 -> worst case 4e10 operations)\n    \n    # Alternative: precompute for each component, the set of forbidden pairs that have one end in it?\n    # But better: for each component, store which forbidden pairs are incident to it?\n    # But we cannot store all forbidden pairs per component if K is large.\n    \n    # We can instead: for each component, store the set of all forbidden pairs that are split across the components?\n    # Actually: we want: for any two components A and B, is there a forbidden pair (x,y) such that x in A and y in B?\n    # Then if the query adds an edge between A and B, and there exists such forbidden pair, then it's bad.\n    \n    # But note: we cannot precompute all pairs of components, because there are up to N components.\n    \n    # Instead: we can precompute for each component, the set of \"forbidden\" vertices that are endpoints of forbidden edges?\n    # Actually: we want: for a query (p, q), if comp_p != comp_q, then we check: is there any forbidden pair (x, y) with x in comp_p and y in comp_q?\n    # We can precompute a dictionary: for each component, store the set of forbidden pairs that have one end in this component?\n    # But we want to know if there exists at least one forbidden pair between component A and component B.\n    \n    # Let's do: create a set for each component of the forbidden pairs that are incident to vertices in that component?\n    # But then to check between two components A and B, we would need to check all forbidden pairs involving A and B? -> too slow.\n    \n    # Alternative: create a set of all forbidden pairs, but map by component pair.\n    # Let's create a dictionary: forbidden_edges_by_component_pair = {}\n    # But we don't want to store duplicates.\n    # We can do: create a set of tuples (min(comp_x, comp_y), max(comp_x, comp_y)) for each forbidden pair (x, y) that has x and y in different components.\n    \n    # Since original graph is good, no forbidden pair is in same component -> all are in different components.\n    # So for each forbidden pair (x, y), we get (min(c_x, c_y), max(c_x, c_y)) and store in a set.\n    \n    # Then for a query (p, q):\n    #   if comp_p == comp_q: -> no new connection -> no forbidden pair becomes connected -> \"Yes\"\n    #   else: let a = min(comp_p, comp_q), b = max(comp_p, comp_q)\n    #         if (a, b) is in the set of forbidden component pairs -> then there is a forbidden pair (x,y) with x in a and y in b -> after adding edge, they become connected -> \"No\"\n    #         else: \"Yes\"\n    \n    # But wait: what if multiple forbidden pairs exist between the same pair of components? we only need one to make it bad.\n    # So we use a set: if (a,b) is in the set, then at least one forbidden pair lies across this component pair -> bad.\n    \n    # Step: build a set of component pairs (min_comp, max_comp) for each forbidden pair\n    forbidden_component_pairs = set()\n    \n    for x, y in forbidden:\n        comp_x = comp_map[x]\n        comp_y = comp_map[y]\n        if comp_x == comp_y:\n            # This should not happen per problem, but just in case\n            # But problem says \"For all i=1,...,K, there is no path connecting x_i and y_i\"\n            # So they must be in different components\n            continue\n        a, b = min(comp_x, comp_y), max(comp_x, comp_y)\n        forbidden_component_pairs.add((a, b))\n    \n    # Now answer each query\n    results = []\n    for p, q in queries:\n        comp_p = comp_map[p]\n        comp_q = comp_map[q]\n        if comp_p == comp_q:\n            # Adding an edge within same component: no new connection -> still good\n            results.append(\"Yes\")\n        else:\n            a, b = min(comp_p, comp_q), max(comp_p, comp_q)\n            if (a, b) in forbidden_component_pairs:\n                results.append(\"No\")\n            else:\n                results.append(\"Yes\")\n    \n    # Output results\n    for res in results:\n        print(res)\n\n# Call the function\nsolve()\n```",
