      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef solve():\n    MOD = 998244353\n    \n    def mod_inv(a, mod):\n        return pow(a, mod - 2, mod)\n    \n    def dfs(grid, visited, i, j, h, w):\n        if i < 0 or i >= h or j < 0 or j >= w or visited[i][j] or grid[i][j] != '#':\n            return\n        visited[i][j] = True\n        for di, dj in [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)]:\n            dfs(grid, visited, i + di, j + dj, h, w)\n    \n    # Read input\n    H, W = map(int, input().split())\n    grid = []\n    for _ in range(H):\n        grid.append(list(input().strip()))\n    \n    # Count total red cells\n    total_red = 0\n    red_positions = []\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '.':\n                total_red += 1\n                red_positions.append((i, j))\n    \n    # Count initial green connected components\n    visited = [[False] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    initial_components = 0\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '#' and not visited[i][j]:\n                dfs(grid, visited, i, j, H, W)\n                initial_components += 1\n    \n    # For each red cell, compute the resulting number of components after painting it green\n    total_components = 0\n    \n    for ri, rj in red_positions:\n        # Clone grid and mark this red cell as green temporarily\n        # But we don't need to actually clone, just simulate\n        # Instead, we need to check how this cell connects to existing green cells\n        # and how many components it connects to\n        \n        # Find all adjacent green cells\n        adjacent_components = set()\n        neighbor_counts = {}\n        \n        for di, dj in [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)]:\n            ni, nj = ri + di, rj + dj\n            if 0 <= ni < H and 0 <= nj < W and grid[ni][nj] == '#':\n                # Find the component id of this green neighbor\n                # But since we can't modify visited, we need to recompute component id for the whole grid with this cell?\n                # Actually, we can do: simulate connecting to neighbors and see how many unique components they belong to.\n                pass\n                \n        # Reuse the visited array and find the component ID for each green neighbor\n        comp_id = [[-1] * W for _ in range(H)]\n        comp_count = 0\n        for i in range(H):\n            for j in range(W):\n                if grid[i][j] == '#' and comp_id[i][j] == -1:\n                    # BFS/DFS to assign component id\n                    queue = deque([(i, j)])\n                    comp_id[i][j] = comp_count\n                    while queue:\n                        x, y = queue.popleft()\n                        for di, dj in [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)]:\n                            nx, ny = x + di, y + dj\n                            if 0 <= nx < H and 0 <= ny < W and grid[nx][ny] == '#' and comp_id[nx][ny] == -1:\n                                comp_id[nx][ny] = comp_count\n                                queue.append((nx, ny))\n                    comp_count += 1\n        \n        # Now, find the component id of each adjacent green cell\n        adj_comps = set()\n        for di, dj in [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)]:\n            ni, nj = ri + di, rj + dj\n            if 0 <= ni < H and 0 <= nj < W and grid[ni][nj] == '#':\n                adj_comps.add(comp_id[ni][nj])\n        \n        # Number of components this cell connects to\n        num_new_components = len(adj_comps)\n        \n        # Final components = initial_components - num_new_components + 1\n        # Because we're merging num_new_components components into one (via the new green cell)\n        final_components = initial_components - num_new_components + 1\n        \n        total_components += final_components\n    \n    # Expected value = total_components / total_red\n    # We want: (total_components * mod_inv(total_red, MOD)) % MOD\n    result = (total_components * mod_inv(total_red, MOD)) % MOD\n    print(result)\n\nsolve()\n```",
