    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_177",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed string word and an integer k.\nAt every second, you must perform the following operations:\n\nRemove the first k characters of word.\nAdd any k characters to the end of word.\n\nNote that you do not necessarily need to add the same characters that you removed. However, you must perform both operations at every second.\nReturn the minimum time greater than zero required for word to revert to its initial state.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: word = \"abacaba\", k = 3\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: At the 1st second, we remove characters \"aba\" from the prefix of word, and add characters \"bac\" to the end of word. Thus, word becomes equal to \"cababac\".\nAt the 2nd second, we remove characters \"cab\" from the prefix of word, and add \"aba\" to the end of word. Thus, word becomes equal to \"abacaba\" and reverts to its initial state.\nIt can be shown that 2 seconds is the minimum time greater than zero required for word to revert to its initial state.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: word = \"abacaba\", k = 4\nOutput: 1\nExplanation: At the 1st second, we remove characters \"abac\" from the prefix of word, and add characters \"caba\" to the end of word. Thus, word becomes equal to \"abacaba\" and reverts to its initial state.\nIt can be shown that 1 second is the minimum time greater than zero required for word to revert to its initial state.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: word = \"abcbabcd\", k = 2\nOutput: 4\nExplanation: At every second, we will remove the first 2 characters of word, and add the same characters to the end of word.\nAfter 4 seconds, word becomes equal to \"abcbabcd\" and reverts to its initial state.\nIt can be shown that 4 seconds is the minimum time greater than zero required for word to revert to its initial state.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= word.length <= 50 \n1 <= k <= word.length\nword consists only of lowercase English letters.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-30b-a3b-instruct-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minimumTime(word: str, k: int) -> int:\n    n = len(word)\n    time = 1\n    \n    # We simulate the process second by second\n    # At each second, we remove first k characters and add any k characters to the end\n    # We want to find the minimum time > 0 when the word returns to initial state\n    \n    # After each second, the remaining part of word is word[k:], and we can add any k characters\n    # So the key idea: if after some seconds, the remaining suffix of the current word\n    # matches a prefix of the original word of the same length, and the rest is filled with\n    # characters that make the whole word equal to the original, then it reverts.\n    \n    # Since we can add any k characters at the end, the word reverts to the original\n    # if and only if the remaining suffix of the current word matches the prefix of the\n    # original word of length (n - k * time), and the rest is filled appropriately.\n    \n    # We can check at each time step: after time seconds, we have removed k * time characters\n    # So the current word starts at index k*time, and we have a suffix of length n - k*time\n    # For the word to revert to initial state, this suffix must match the prefix of the original\n    # word of the same length.\n    \n    # But we can also add any characters to the end, so only the remaining suffix of the current\n    # word needs to match the corresponding prefix of original word.\n    \n    # So the condition is:\n    # word[k*time:] == word[:n - k*time]\n    # and n - k*time >= 0\n    \n    while k * time < n:\n        if word[k * time:] == word[:n - k * time]:\n            return time\n        time += 1\n    \n    # If we've removed all characters, the word becomes empty, then we add back original word\n    # So after n // k + (1 if n % k else 0) seconds, if n % k == 0, then exactly n // k seconds?\n    # But if k * time >= n, then we can reconstruct the word completely at that point\n    \n    # Actually, if k * time >= n, then we have removed all characters, and we can add back\n    # the entire original word. So after time seconds where k * time >= n, we can set the\n    # word to be exactly the original word by adding original word.\n    # So we just need to find the minimum time such that k * time >= n and the suffix matches\n    # the prefix of original word.\n    \n    # But since the condition word[k*time:] == word[:n - k*time] is vacuously true when k*time >= n?\n    # Actually when k*time >= n, then word[k*time:] is empty, and word[:n - k*time] is also empty\n    # since n - k*time <= 0, so we take word[:0] which is empty.\n    # So empty == empty => True.\n    # Therefore, the first time such that k*time >= n will satisfy the condition.\n    \n    # So we return the smallest time such that k*time >= n, which is ceil(n/k)\n    \n    # But wait: we are checking the condition only for k*time < n in the loop. After the loop,\n    # we are guaranteed k*time >= n, so the condition holds.\n    \n    # Therefore, we return the smallest time such that k*time >= n, i.e., ceiling of n/k.\n    \n    # However, we must ensure that we don't miss the case where k*time == n exactly.\n    # So the answer is ceiling(n/k) = (n + k - 1) // k\n    \n    # But in the loop we only break when k*time >= n, and we return time only when condition holds in loop.\n    # If loop doesn't return, then we return the time when k*time >= n.\n    \n    # So:\n    return (n + k - 1) // k\n```",
