    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed string word and an integer k.\nAt every second, you must perform the following operations:\n\nRemove the first k characters of word.\nAdd any k characters to the end of word.\n\nNote that you do not necessarily need to add the same characters that you removed. However, you must perform both operations at every second.\nReturn the minimum time greater than zero required for word to revert to its initial state.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: word = \"abacaba\", k = 3\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: At the 1st second, we remove characters \"aba\" from the prefix of word, and add characters \"bac\" to the end of word. Thus, word becomes equal to \"cababac\".\nAt the 2nd second, we remove characters \"cab\" from the prefix of word, and add \"aba\" to the end of word. Thus, word becomes equal to \"abacaba\" and reverts to its initial state.\nIt can be shown that 2 seconds is the minimum time greater than zero required for word to revert to its initial state.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: word = \"abacaba\", k = 4\nOutput: 1\nExplanation: At the 1st second, we remove characters \"abac\" from the prefix of word, and add characters \"caba\" to the end of word. Thus, word becomes equal to \"abacaba\" and reverts to its initial state.\nIt can be shown that 1 second is the minimum time greater than zero required for word to revert to its initial state.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: word = \"abcbabcd\", k = 2\nOutput: 4\nExplanation: At every second, we will remove the first 2 characters of word, and add the same characters to the end of word.\nAfter 4 seconds, word becomes equal to \"abcbabcd\" and reverts to its initial state.\nIt can be shown that 4 seconds is the minimum time greater than zero required for word to revert to its initial state.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= word.length <= 50 \n1 <= k <= word.length\nword consists only of lowercase English letters.",
    "prediction": "mistralai/ministral-3b-2512",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Here's the Python function body that solves the problem as described:\n\n```python\ndef min_time_to_revert(word: str, k: int) -> int:\n    n = len(word)\n    if k == 1:\n        # The word must be a palindrome to revert in 1 step\n        return 1 if word == word[::-1] else 2 * (n + 1)\n\n    # Check if the word can be reverted in 1 step\n    if k == n:\n        return 1\n\n    # Check if the word can be reverted in 2 steps\n    # We need to find a rotation that brings the word back to original\n    # by removing first k chars and adding k chars from the end\n    # The condition is: word[k:] + word[:k] == word\n    if (word[k:] + word[:k]) == word:\n        return 2\n\n    # For cases where we need more steps, we need to find the minimal period\n    # where the word repeats after some operations\n    # We can model this as finding the minimal t where:\n    # word is equal to word rotated by k characters t times\n    # This is equivalent to finding the minimal t where:\n    # word is a rotation of itself by k characters t times\n    # which is equivalent to finding the minimal t where:\n    # word is a rotation of itself by (t*k) mod n characters\n    # So we need to find the minimal t > 0 where:\n    # word == word[ (t*k) % n : ] + word[ : (t*k) % n ]\n\n    # We can find this by checking all possible t from 1 to n\n    # until we find the minimal t where the condition holds\n    for t in range(1, n + 1):\n        if word == word[(t * k) % n :] + word[:(t * k) % n]:\n            return t\n\n    # If no such t found (shouldn't happen for valid inputs)\n    return n\n```\n\nThis function:\n1. Handles special cases where k=1 or k=n directly\n2. Checks if the word can be reverted in 1 or 2 steps\n3. For other cases, finds the minimal time t where applying the operation t times brings the word back to its original state\n4. The solution works by checking all possible rotations until finding the minimal valid t\n\nThe function doesn't invoke itself (no recursion) and follows the problem constraints efficiently.",
