    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are at a fruit market with different types of exotic fruits on display.\nYou are given a 1-indexed array prices, where prices[i] denotes the number of coins needed to purchase the i^th fruit.\nThe fruit market has the following offer:\n\nIf you purchase the i^th fruit at prices[i] coins, you can get the next i fruits for free.\n\nNote that even if you can take fruit j for free, you can still purchase it for prices[j] coins to receive a new offer.\nReturn the minimum number of coins needed to acquire all the fruits.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: prices = [3,1,2]\nOutput: 4\nExplanation: You can acquire the fruits as follows:\n- Purchase the 1^st fruit with 3 coins, you are allowed to take the 2^nd fruit for free.\n- Purchase the 2^nd fruit with 1 coin, you are allowed to take the 3^rd fruit for free.\n- Take the 3^rd fruit for free.\nNote that even though you were allowed to take the 2^nd fruit for free, you purchased it because it is more optimal.\nIt can be proven that 4 is the minimum number of coins needed to acquire all the fruits.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: prices = [1,10,1,1]\nOutput: 2\nExplanation: You can acquire the fruits as follows:\n- Purchase the 1^st fruit with 1 coin, you are allowed to take the 2^nd fruit for free.\n- Take the 2^nd fruit for free.\n- Purchase the 3^rd fruit for 1 coin, you are allowed to take the 4^th fruit for free.\n- Take the 4^t^h fruit for free.\nIt can be proven that 2 is the minimum number of coins needed to acquire all the fruits.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= prices.length <= 1000\n1 <= prices[i] <= 10^5",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-30b-a3b-instruct-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef minimumCoins(prices):\n    n = len(prices)\n    # dp[i] represents the minimum coins needed to acquire fruits from index i to n-1\n    dp = [float('inf')] * (n + 1)\n    dp[n] = 0  # Base case: no fruits left to buy\n    \n    # Fill dp array from right to left\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        # We must buy the i-th fruit (1-indexed, so index i corresponds to i+1)\n        # When we buy fruit i, we get the next i fruits (1-indexed) for free\n        # So we can skip from i+1 to min(i + (i+1), n) = min(2*i+2, n)\n        # But note: since i is 0-indexed, buying at index i gives free access to next (i+1) fruits\n        # so we can skip up to index i + (i+1) = 2*i+1 (0-indexed)\n        \n        max_free = min(2 * i + 1, n - 1)  # Last free index (0-indexed)\n        \n        # Option 1: buy the current fruit and get next (i+1) fruits for free\n        # So we pay prices[i] and then start from max_free + 1\n        if max_free + 1 <= n:\n            dp[i] = prices[i] + dp[max_free + 1]\n        else:\n            dp[i] = prices[i]  # Only need to buy current fruit\n                \n        # Option 2: we could still buy the current fruit even if we could get it free?\n        # But the key is: we can *choose* to pay to get the offer. So we can always choose to buy\n        # So the above option already considers the best case: we buy now, gain offer, skip next\n        # But what if we don’t buy now but later want to skip? Our dp state transitions handle that.\n        \n        # Actually, we should also consider the possibility that we might want to buy the next fruit\n        # to get another offer, but since we're doing DP from right to left, we already consider\n        # the future states.\n        \n        # Actually, we might not have considered buying at i but then starting after the free range.\n        # Our initial calculation above is correct: buying at i gives free access to fruits i+1 to i+(i+1)\n        # i.e., indices i+1 to min(2*i+1, n-1) (0-indexed)\n        # So we pay prices[i] and then the cost from max_free+1 onward.\n    \n    return dp[0]\n```",
