use one (and we can choose which one to use, but it doesn't matter).\n    #   if we don't have one, we fail.\n    # But we can choose whether to pick up a potion when we find it.\n    # Strategy: postpone picking up potions as long as possible? \n    # However, we want to minimize the peak.\n    \n    # Insight: we should only pick up a potion if there is a future monster of that type that we cannot defeat otherwise.\n    # We can precompute the number of future monsters of each type.\n    \n    # Precompute future monster counts\n    from collections import Counter\n    monster_count = Counter()\n    for t, x in events:\n        if t == 2:\n            monster_count[x] += 1\n    \n    # We'll simulate and greedily decide when to pick up\n    # At each event:\n    #   if t == 1: we can choose to pick it up (add to inventory) or discard\n    #   if t == 2: we must have a potion of type x to defeat it\n    # We want to minimize the maximum inventory size.\n    \n    # We'll try a greedy: pick up a potion only if we don't already have enough potions of that type\n    # to cover all future monsters of that type? But we might use a potion from a different time.\n    \n    # Actually, standard solution: use the \"greedy with backward simulation\"\n    # We can use a different approach: we can simulate the entire process with a greedy policy:\n    # When we get a potion, if we already have K potions, we don't pick it up.\n    # But we don't know K in advance.\n    \n    # We'll do binary search on K (the maximum number of potions at any time)\n    # For a fixed K, can we complete the adventure?\n    # We simulate with the following policy:\n    #   Maintain a multiset (or array) of current potions.\n    #   For each event i:\n    #       if t_i == 1: if we have less than K potions, we pick it up; otherwise, we discard it.\n    #       if t_i == 2: if we have at least one potion of type x_i, we use one; otherwise, we fail.\n    # But wait, this is not optimal: we might want