    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Here's the Python function body for the given problem:\n\n```python\ndef maxFrequency(nums, k):\n    nums.sort()\n    max_freq = 1\n    current_freq = 1\n    left = 0\n\n    for right in range(1, len(nums)):\n        # Calculate the cost to make all elements between left and right equal to nums[right]\n        cost = nums[right] * (right - left) - sum(nums[left:right+1])\n\n        if cost <= k:\n            k -= cost\n            current_freq += 1\n        else:\n            # Try to move left pointer to reduce the cost\n            while left <= right and cost > k:\n                cost -= nums[left] - nums[right]\n                left += 1\n            if left <= right:\n                current_freq = right - left + 1\n                k -= cost\n\n        max_freq = max(max_freq, current_freq)\n\n    return max_freq\n```\n\nThis function implements a sliding window approach to solve the problem efficiently. It sorts the array first and then tries to maximize the frequency of the most frequent element by considering how many operations are needed to make all elements in a window equal to the rightmost element in that window. The key insight is to balance between the cost of making elements equal and the number of operations allowed.",
