][y + dy] = pos + 1\n                        # Recurse\n                        if can_place(grid, used_tiles | {pos}, pos + 1):\n                            return True\n                        # Backtrack\n                        for dx in range(a):\n                            for dy in range(b):\n                                grid[x + dx][y + dy] = 0\n        return False\n\n    # Initialize grid with 0 (empty)\n    grid = [[0 for _ in range(W)] for _ in range(H)]\n    if can_place(grid, set(), 0):\n        print(\"Yes\")\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n1. **Input Reading**: The function reads all input at once for efficiency, especially given the constraints.\n2. **Area Check**: It first checks if the total area of the tiles is at least the grid area. If not, it immediately returns \"No\".\n3. **Backtracking**: The `can_place` function uses backtracking to try placing each tile in all possible orientations and positions:\n   - For each tile, it tries both orientations (original and rotated).\n   - For each valid position (where the tile fits within the grid and doesn't overlap with already placed tiles), it places the tile and recursively checks if the remaining tiles can cover the rest of the grid.\n   - If a valid configuration is found, it returns \"Yes\"; otherwise, it returns \"No\".\n\nThis approach ensures that all possible configurations are explored, and the solution is found if one exists. The constraints (N ≤ 7, H, W ≤ 10) make this feasible with backtracking.",
