      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef canTraverseAllPairs(nums):\n    if len(nums) == 1:\n        return True\n    \n    # If there's a 1 in the array, we can't traverse to or from it unless it's the only element\n    if 1 in nums:\n        return len(nums) == 1\n    \n    # Use union-find to group indices that can be connected\n    parent = list(range(len(nums)))\n    \n    def find(x):\n        if parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x])\n        return parent[x]\n    \n    def union(x, y):\n        px, py = find(x), find(y)\n        if px != py:\n            parent[px] = py\n    \n    # For each pair of indices, check if they can be connected\n    # Instead of checking all pairs, we optimize by checking prime factors\n    # Group indices by their prime factors\n    factor_to_indices = {}\n    \n    for i, num in enumerate(nums):\n        temp = num\n        d = 2\n        while d * d <= temp:\n            if temp % d == 0:\n                while temp % d == 0:\n                    temp //= d\n                if d not in factor_to_indices:\n                    factor_to_indices[d] = []\n                factor_to_indices[d].append(i)\n            d += 1\n        if temp > 1:\n            if temp not in factor_to_indices:\n                factor_to_indices[temp] = []\n            factor_to_indices[temp].append(i)\n    \n    # Union all indices that share a common prime factor\n    for indices in factor_to_indices.values():\n        for j in range(1, len(indices)):\n            union(indices[0], indices[j])\n    \n    # Check if all indices are in the same connected component\n    root = find(0)\n    for i in range(1, len(nums)):\n        if find(i) != root:\n            return False\n    \n    return True\n```",
