      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef get_min_distance(c1, c2):\n    # Calculate minimum cyclic distance between two characters\n    diff = abs(ord(c1) - ord(c2))\n    return min(diff, 26 - diff)\n\ndef smallestString(s, k):\n    n = len(s)\n    s_list = list(s)\n    \n    # We will try to make the string lexicographically smallest\n    # by changing characters from left to right\n    \n    remaining_k = k\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        if remaining_k == 0:\n            break\n            \n        # Current character\n        current_char = s_list[i]\n        \n        # Try to change to 'a' if possible (lexicographically smallest)\n        distance_to_a = get_min_distance(current_char, 'a')\n        \n        if distance_to_a <= remaining_k:\n            s_list[i] = 'a'\n            remaining_k -= distance_to_a\n        else:\n            # Cannot change to 'a', but we can try to minimize by moving in the shortest direction\n            # We need to reduce the distance by exactly 'remaining_k'\n            # We can move forward or backward in the cyclic order\n            current_ord = ord(current_char) - ord('a')\n            \n            # Option 1: Move backward (toward 'a') by remaining_k steps\n            new_ord = (current_ord - remaining_k) % 26\n            new_char = chr(new_ord + ord('a'))\n            \n            # Option 2: Move forward (away from 'a') by remaining_k steps\n            # This would be worse lexicographically, so we avoid it\n            # But we need to check which gives minimum distance\n            # Actually: we want the smallest character possible, so moving backward is better\n            \n            # Since we want lexicographically smallest, and moving backward gives smaller char\n            s_list[i] = chr(new_ord + ord('a'))\n            \n            # No need to subtract k here because we already used remaining_k to move\n            remaining_k = 0\n    \n    return ''.join(s_list)\n```",
