>= n, which is true because s+1 = (n+k-1)//k >= n/k => k*(s+1) >= n.\n    #\n    # Actually, the necessary and sufficient condition for k groups to work is:\n    # Let s = n // k, r = n % k.\n    # Then the k groups must consist of r groups of size s+1 and (k - r) groups of size s.\n    #\n    # Now, for each value with frequency f, we can split it into groups of size at most s+1 (the max group size), so we need at least ceil(f / (s+1)) groups for that value.\n    #\n    # But also, we cannot use more than r groups of size s+1 and k - r groups of size s.\n    #\n    # So we need to know: can we assign the frequencies to k groups with the given size constraints?\n    #\n    # Alternate idea: we can try every possible k from 1 to n, and check whether k groups suffice.\n    # For a given k:\n    #   - s = n // k\n    #   - r = n % k   -> r groups of size s+1, k - r groups of size s\n    #   - maximum number of elements that can be put in groups of size s+1: r * (s+1)\n    #   - maximum elements in groups of size s: (k - r) * s\n    #   - total: r*(s+1) + (k-r)*s = k*s + r = n, so it's tight.\n    #\n    # But the constraint is: we can't exceed the group sizes, and each group is for one value.\n    #\n    # So we need to assign each frequency f to one or more groups such that:\n    #   - Each group assigned to a value has size at most s+1\n    #   - The total number of groups used is k, with exactly r groups of size s+1 and k - r groups of size s\n    #\n    # But we are free to choose how we split each frequency.\n    #\n    # The total number of groups used must be k, so we must use exactly k groups.\n    # So we need to assign the frequencies to k groups, each group being assigned to one value, and the size of the group (number of indices) is at most s+1, and we can't exceed the group count per size.\n    #\n    # But it's simpler: we can compute the minimum number of groups needed for each value given that each group can hold at most s+1 elements:\n    #   min_group