      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read input\n    H, W, N = map(int, input().split())\n    T = input().strip()\n    grid = []\n    for _ in range(H):\n        grid.append(input().strip())\n    \n    # All perimeter cells are sea, so we don't need to worry about going out of bounds\n    # But we can only move on land (.)\n    \n    # We'll use reverse simulation: instead of trying all possible starting positions,\n    # we'll find all possible ending positions by reversing the path.\n    # But actually, the easier way is: try all possible starting positions that are land,\n    # and simulate the full path. If the entire path stays on land, then the final position\n    # is valid.\n    \n    # However, we don't know the starting position. We must consider that the starting\n    # position could be any land cell such that the entire path (N+1 steps) stays on land.\n    \n    # But note: the constraints say that all perimeter cells are sea, so starting positions\n    # must be in the inner part (2 to H-1 and 2 to W-1).\n    \n    # Let's do forward simulation from every possible starting position that is land?\n    # But H, W up to 500 -> 250000 cells. N up to 500 -> 250000*500 = 125e6 which might be too slow in Python.\n    \n    # Instead, we can use a smarter method: BFS or DP from all land cells, but only keeping\n    # positions that are reachable at each step.\n    \n    # We'll use a set of possible positions at each step.\n    # Start with all land cells that could be starting points. But note: the starting point\n    # must be such that after following T, the entire path is on land.\n    \n    # Actually: the problem says that \"all cells along his path (including the crash-landed \n    # and current position) are not sea\". So we only care about paths that stay on land.\n    # We are to count how many distinct final positions could be reached from some starting\n    # land cell such that the entire path is on land.\n    \n    # We can simulate backwards: the final position must be a land cell, and we can reverse\n    # the move sequence to find all possible starting positions. Then check if that starting\n    # position is land and if the path is valid.\n    \n    # But easier: use dynamic programming with set of possible positions at each step.\n    # Let S[i] be the set of positions (r, c) such that it's possible to be at that position\n    # after i moves.\n    \n    # Initially: S[0] = set of all land cells that are not on the perimeter (but perimeter is already sea, so any land cell is fine).\n    # Actually: the problem says all perimeter cells are sea. So any land cell is not on perimeter.\n    \n    # So S[0] = all land cells (r, c) such that grid[r][c] == '.'.\n    \n    # Then for each move in T:\n    #   S[i+1] = set of positions reachable from S[i] by one move in direction T[i]\n    #   But only if the move stays on the grid and lands on land.\n    #   But also: we must check that the path remains on land at each step.\n    \n    # However, note: the move is determined by T. So for each position in S[i], we try to move\n    # in direction T[i]. If the new cell is within bounds and is '.', then it's valid.\n    \n    # But we must only consider paths that are consistent with the move string T.\n    \n    # Let's do:\n    positions = set()\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '.':\n                positions.add((i, j))\n    \n    # Now simulate N moves\n    for direction in T:\n        next_positions = set()\n        for r, c in positions:\n            dr, dc = 0, 0\n            if direction == 'L': dc = -1\n            elif direction == 'R': dc = 1\n            elif direction == 'U': dr = -1\n            elif direction == 'D': dr = 1\n            \n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            # Check bounds and if the new cell is land\n            if 0 <= nr < H and 0 <= nc < W and grid[nr][nc] == '.':\n                next_positions.add((nr, nc))\n        positions = next_positions\n        # Early termination: if no positions, break (but problem says at least one exists)\n        if not positions:\n            break\n    \n    # Answer is the number of possible current positions\n    print(len(positions))\n\n# Call the function\nsolve()\n```",
