      "generated_answer": "Here's the executable Python function based on the given problem:\n\n```python\ndef max_jumps_to_reach_end(nums, target):\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n == 1:\n        return 0\n\n    jumps = [0] * n\n    visited = [False] * n\n\n    # We'll use BFS to explore all possible paths\n    from collections import deque\n    queue = deque()\n    queue.append((0, 0))  # (current index, number of jumps)\n    visited[0] = True\n\n    while queue:\n        current_index, current_jumps = queue.popleft()\n\n        if current_index == n - 1:\n            return current_jumps\n\n        for next_index in range(current_index + 1, n):\n            if not visited[next_index] and abs(nums[next_index] - nums[current_index]) <= target:\n                visited[next_index] = True\n                queue.append((next_index, current_jumps + 1))\n\n    return -1\n```\n\nHowever, upon closer inspection, the BFS approach above doesn't guarantee the maximum jumps because it explores all possible paths level by level (which would give the shortest path in terms of jumps, not the longest). For the maximum jumps, we need a different approach.\n\nHere's a corrected version that uses dynamic programming to track the maximum jumps:\n\n```python\ndef max_jumps_to_reach_end(nums, target):\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n == 1:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i] will store the maximum jumps to reach index i\n    dp = [-1] * n\n    dp[0] = 0  # Starting at index 0 with 0 jumps\n\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        if dp[i] == -1:\n            continue  # Cannot reach this index\n\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            if abs(nums[j] - nums[i]) <= target:\n                if dp[j] < dp[i] + 1:\n                    dp[j] = dp[i] + 1\n\n    return dp[-1] if dp[-1] != -1 else -1\n```\n\nThis dynamic programming approach correctly tracks the maximum number of jumps by updating the maximum jumps to each index based on all possible previous jumps that satisfy the condition.",
