    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The most likely correct answer is **G. is more objective, since it does not deal with the strengths and weaknesses of the employee**.\n\nForced-choice methods force raters to choose between predefined pairs of behaviors or traits, reducing the tendency to rate employees uniformly (e.g., leniency or severity) and thus improving objectivity. This approach also mitigates the \"halo effect\" by requiring explicit comparisons rather than broad generalizations. While other options (like reliability or simplicity) have merits, the core advantage lies in its structured, comparative nature.",
