      "generated_answer": "We are given three premises:  \n1. I ⊃ (J ≡ K)  \n2. (J ≡ K) ⊃ (L ∨ M)  \n3. I ⊃ ~L  \n\nWe are to determine which option is **not** an immediate (one-step) consequence of these premises.\n\nOption A (~L): Not immediately followable — we only know I ⊃ ~L, so ~L only follows if I is true, which we don’t know.  \nOption B (J ≡ K): Not directly followable unless I is true, so not immediate.  \nOption C (I ⊃ (L ∨ M)): Can be derived via hypothetical syllogism from (1) and (2): I ⊃ (J ≡ K) and (J ≡ K) ⊃ (L ∨ M) gives I ⊃ (L ∨ M). So this is an immediate consequence.  \nOption D (~L ⊃ (J ≡ K)): This is not derivable in one step from the premises; it requires a non-sequitur or reverse reasoning.\n\nThe correct answer is **D**, as it is not a one-step logical consequence.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
