      "generated_answer": "The sentence states that Brittany likes both fish and lizards, and also does not like cats. This requires a conjunction of two parts: (likes fish and lizards) and (does not like cats). Option B, (F • L) • ~C, correctly groups the conjunction of F and L, then conjoins it with ~C. This matches the logical structure of the sentence.\n\nOption A is incorrect because it treats all three as separate conjuncts without proper grouping, which could lead to ambiguity. Option C uses a disjunction (∨), which is incorrect since the sentence is not about either fish/lizards or not cats, but about both together. Option D wrongly places the negation of cats first, suggesting cats are the main focus, which distorts the meaning.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
