    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which statement best explains the purpose of Hart's distinction between 'being obliged' and 'having an obligation'?\n\nOptions: \nA. It refutes the natural lawyer' view of the role of morality in law.\nB. It illuminates the concept of a rule.\nC. It differentiates between formal and substantive justice.\nD. It highlights the importance of individual rights in law.\nE. It clarifies the relationship between law and society.\nF. It explains the nature of power-conferring rules.\nG. It distinguishes between legal and ethical obligations.\nH. It provides a guideline for the application of laws.\nI. It explains the role of judicial discretion in the law.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-30b-a3b-instruct-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The distinction between 'being obliged' and 'having an obligation' in H.L.A. Hart's theory is meant to clarify how legal rules function: one can be legally obliged (i.e., required by law) without necessarily having an obligation in the moral sense. This distinction helps separate legal duties from moral duties, emphasizing that law's validity does not depend on morality. Thus, the primary purpose is to clarify the difference between legal and ethical obligations.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
