    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We are given that $ n + 2 $ divides $ 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) $. Let $ d = n + 2 $, so $ n = d - 2 $. Substitute into the expression:\n\n$$\n3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3((d - 2) + 3)((d - 2)^2 + 9) = 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 4 + 9) = 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13)\n$$\n\nSo $ d \\mid 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) $. Since $ d $ divides this expression, and $ d $ and $ d+1 $ are coprime, we analyze divisibility conditions.\n\nWe now check small positive integer values of $ d $ (since $ d = n + 2 > 2 $, and $ n > 0 $), and test when $ d $ divides $ 3(d + 1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) $. We systematically compute.\n\nTry $ d = 1 $: $ n = -1 $, not positive.  \n$ d = 2 $: $ n = 0 $, not positive.  \n$ d = 3 $: $ n = 1 $. Check: $ n+2=3 $, product $ 3(4)(1+9) = 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 10 = 120 $, $ 3 \\mid 120 $? Yes. So $ n = 1 $ valid.  \n$ d = 4 $: $ n = 2 $. $ 3(5)(4+9)=3\\cdot5\\cdot13=195 $, $ 4 \\mid 195 $? No.  \n$ d = 5 $: $ n = 3 $. $ 3(6)(9+9)=3\\cdot6\\cdot18=324 $, $ 5 \\mid 324 $? No.  \n$ d = 6 $: $ n = 4 $. $ 3(7)(16+9)=3\\cdot7\\cdot25=525 $, $ 6 \\mid 525 $? No.  \n$ d = 7 $: $ n = 5 $. $ 3(8)(25+9)=3\\cdot8\\cdot34=816 $, $ 7 \\mid 816 $? $ 816 / 7 \\approx 116.57 $, no.  \n$ d = 9 $: $ n = 7 $. $ 3(10)(49+9)=3\\cdot10\\cdot58=1740 $, $ 9 \\mid 1740 $? $ 1+7+4+0=12 $, not divisible by 9. No.  \n$ d = 13 $: $ n = 11 $. Compute $ 3(14)(121+9)=3\\cdot14\\cdot130=5460 $, $ 13 \\mid 5460 $? $ 5460 / 13 = 420 $, yes. So $ n = 11 $ valid.  \n$ d = 39 $: $ n = 37 $. Try: $ 3(40)(37^2 + 9) = 3\\cdot40\\cdot(1369 + 9) = 120 \\cdot 1378 = 165360 $. Is $ 39 \\mid 165360 $? $ 39 = 3\\cdot13 $. $ 165360 / 13 = 12720 $, $ 12720 / 3 = 4240 $, yes. So $ n = 37 $ valid.\n\nNow check $ d = 3, 13, 39 $, giving $ n = 1, 11, 37 $. Try $ d = 39 \\Rightarrow n = 37 $. Try $ d = 39 \\cdot 13 = 507 $? Probably too big. Assume only finite solutions.\n\nCheck divisibility: since $ d \\mid 3(d+1)(d^2 - 4d + 13) $, and $ \\gcd(d, d+1) = 1 $, so $ d \\mid 3(d^2 - 4d + 13) $.\n\nLet $ f(d) = d^2 - 4d + 13 $. Then check when $ d \\mid 3f(d) $. That is,\n\n$$\nd \\mid 3(d^2 - 4d + 13) \\Rightarrow d \\mid 3d^2 - 12d + 39\n$$\n\nBut $ d \\mid 3d^2 $ and $ d \\mid 12d $, so $ d \\mid 39 $. Therefore, $ d \\mid 39 $\n\nSo $ d $ must be a positive divisor of 39: $ d = 1, 3, 13, 39 $\n\n$ d = 1 \\Rightarrow n = -1 $ (invalid),  \n$ d = 3 \\Rightarrow n = 1 $,  \n$ d = 13 \\Rightarrow n = 11 $,  \n$ d = 39 \\Rightarrow n = 37 $\n\nAll valid, since we tested them.\n\nSum: $ 1 + 11 + 37 = 49 $\n\n\\boxed{{49}}",
