\n\n### Step 9: Find Corresponding \\( n \\) Values\nRecall that \\( d = n + 2 \\). So, for each divisor \\( d \\) of 39, we can find \\( n \\):\n\n1. \\( d = 1 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 1 \\implies n = -1 \\]\n   But \\( n \\) must be positive. Discard.\n\n2. \\( d = 3 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 3 \\implies n = 1 \\]\n   Positive integer. Valid.\n\n3. \\( d = 13 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 13 \\implies n = 11 \\]\n   Positive integer. Valid.\n\n4. \\( d = 39 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 39 \\implies n = 37 \\]\n   Positive integer. Valid.\n\n### Verification\nLet's verify each \\( n \\) to ensure \\( n + 2 \\) indeed divides \\( 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) \\).\n\n1. For \\( n = 1 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 3 \\]\n   \\[ 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(4)(1 + 9) = 3 \\times 4 \\times 10 = 120 \\]\n   \\( 3 \\) divides \\( 120 \\). Valid.\n\n2. For \\( n = 11 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 13 \\]\n   \\[ 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(14)(121 + 9) = 3 \\times 14 \\times 130 = 3 \\times 14 \\times 130 = 3 \\times 1820 = 5460 \\]\n   \\( 13 \\) divides \\( 5460 \\) because \\( 5460 / 13 = 420 \\). Valid.\n\n3. For \\( n = 37 \\):\n   \\[ n + 2 = 39 \\]\n   \\[ 3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9) = 3(40)(1369 + 9) = 3 \\times 40 \\times 1378 = 120 \\times 1378 = 165360 \\]\n   \\( 39 \\) divides \\( 165360 \\) because \\( 165360 / 39 = 4238 \\). Valid.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe valid positive integers \\( n \\) are 1, 11, and 37.\n\nNow, sum them up:\n\\[ 1 + 11 + 37 = 49 \\]\n\n### Final Answer\n\\boxed{49}",
