  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7451",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If $m$ and $n$ are odd integers, how many terms in the expansion of $(m+n)^6$ are odd?\n\nOptions: \nA. 6\nB. 3\nC. All the terms are odd\nD. 4\nE. 1\nF. 7\nG. 8\nH. 2\nI. 9\nJ. 5\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-30b-a3b-instruct-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Since $m$ and $n$ are odd integers, any power of $m$ or $n$ remains odd. In the expansion of $(m+n)^6$, each term is of the form $\\binom{6}{k} m^{6-k} n^k$. The parity (odd/even) of the term depends on $\\binom{6}{k}$, because odd $\\times$ odd = odd. So the term is odd if and only if $\\binom{6}{k}$ is odd. The binomial coefficients $\\binom{6}{k}$ for $k = 0$ to $6$ are: 1, 6, 15, 20, 15, 6, 1. Among these, the odd ones are: 1, 15, 15, 1 — that’s 4 terms. So 4 terms in the expansion are odd.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 147,
        "output_tokens": 201,
        "total_tokens": 348
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
