    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10701",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find recursively the sequence y [n] such that y [1] = 3, y [0] = 2, and y [n] - 3y [n - 1] + 2y [n - 2] = 0 ; n \\geq 2.\n\nOptions: \nA. y [n] = 5, 9, 15,...; n >= 2\nB. y [n] = 5, 9, 17,...; n >= 2\nC. y [n] = 2, 5, 9,...; n >= 2\nD. y [n] = 3, 5, 9,...; n >= 2\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-30b-a3b-instruct-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Using the recurrence relation $ y[n] - 3y[n-1] + 2y[n-2] = 0 $ with initial conditions $ y[0] = 2 $, $ y[1] = 3 $, we compute $ y[2] = 3y[1] - 2y[0] = 3(3) - 2(2) = 9 - 4 = 5 $, and $ y[3] = 3y[2] - 2y[1] = 3(5) - 2(3) = 15 - 6 = 9 $. Continuing, $ y[4] = 3(9) - 2(5) = 27 - 10 = 17 $, so the sequence for $ n \\geq 2 $ starts as 5, 9, 17,...\n\nThus, the correct option is **B**.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
