      "generated_answer": "The open-circuit emf of an alternator is calculated using the formula:  \n$$ E = 4.44 \\times f \\times \\phi \\times N \\times k_d \\times k_p $$  \nwhere $ f = 60 $ Hz, $ \\phi = 0.025 $ Wb (2,500,000 maxwells), $ N $ is total number of conductors per phase, $ k_d $ is distribution factor, and $ k_p $ is pitch factor.  \n\nTotal conductors = 6 poles × 12 slots/pole × 4 conductors/slot = 288 conductors. Since Y-connected, conductors per phase = 288 / 3 = 96.  \nPitch factor $ k_p = \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) $, where $ \\alpha = 30^\\circ $ (5/6 pitch of 180°), so $ k_p = \\cos(15^\\circ) \\approx 0.9659 $.  \nDistribution factor $ k_d = \\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{m\\beta}{2}\\right)}{m \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)} $, with $ m = 2 $ (slots per pole per phase), $ \\beta = 30^\\circ $, so $ k_d \\approx 0.9659 $.  \n\nSubstituting:  \n$ E = 4.44 \\times 60 \\times 0.025 \\times 96 \\times 0.9659 \\times 0.9659 \\approx 513 $ volts.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
