    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-30b-a3b-instruct-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The E-B interval is initially an augmented fourth (or tritone), which is 6 semitones. A diminished fifth is also 6 semitones, so the interval size is already correct. To reduce E-B to a diminished fifth, we must lower the B (or raise the E) in ways that preserve the interval size but change the quality to diminished.\n\nHowever, since the augmented fourth and diminished fifth are enharmonically equivalent, they share the same pitch distance but differ in notation. To make E-B a diminished fifth, we must alter the notes such that the interval becomes a fifth (not a fourth) and diminished. This requires moving B down by one semitone (B→B♭), or E up by one semitone (E→E♯), or using double alterations (e.g., B→B𝄫, E→E𝄪), etc.\n\nThere are exactly 3 ways to notate E-B as a diminished fifth: E–B♭, E♯–B♭, or E–B𝄫. Each results in a diminished fifth but uses different accidentals.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 102,
        "output_tokens": 232,
        "total_tokens": 334
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
